"Describes what an organization does by separating transformed inputs, governing controls, produced outputs and enabling mechanisms.\n\nUse when: Scope a business function and its dependencies. Decompose responsibilities without implying a chronological procedure.\n\nAvoid when: The main question is which task happens next or when an event occurs.\n\nVisual grammar: Function boxes carry verb phrases and node numbers. Inputs enter left, controls top, outputs leave right, mechanisms enter below. Child diagrams refine a parent function with balanced boundary arrows.\n\nBuild: Define the viewpoint and purpose in a context diagram. Identify the function's inputs, controls, outputs and mechanisms. Decompose significant functions and reconcile every child boundary with its parent.\n\nCheck: Controls constrain work while inputs are transformed by it. Mechanisms identify resources that enable the function. Decomposition preserves the parent function's meaning and boundary interfaces.\n\nPitfalls: Reading arrow placement as time or execution order. Calling every incoming item an input despite a governing role.\n\nExample: An assess-claim function transforms an application under eligibility policy, using reviewers to produce an approval decision and explanation.\n\nAlternatives: IDEF3 process description, Process classification hierarchy."
